This thread made me think of the magic system in a very old PC game
called Nahlakh.  Spells were entirely based on a 3-syllable structure.
 For example, if you casted a spell and typed in "RIF", it translated
to e.g. "rahk-im-for" on the screen, which loosely translated in
Nahlakh language to "cast small fire".  The result would be something
like a small fireball.  If you typed in "RIX", it translated to
"rahk-im-xi", which was "cast small earth" in English and the result
was like throwing a rock at a monster.

In the game, as you roamed the world and killed things, you sometimes
received a spellbook which showed you the syllables to cast a certain
spell.  But, you could cast any spell without your character learning
it.  If you saw the above examples, you could figure out that "RII"
meant like an ice bolt, or "RMF" meant a bigger fireball, and so on,
and you could cast them without having the spellbook.  But if you
tried to cast something without the correct letters, or without a high
enough skill level, then it wouldn't work and you'd lose mana.

I don't think you could use this exact system in a roguelike without
some modifications.  You'd have to mix up the syllables every game,
since people would otherwise know every spell to begin with.  And, in
the end, this still only provides a finite set of spell effects, since
there are only a finite amount of syllable strings.  However, it would
be a step closer to what Steve was talking about, if it were
implemented in a non-confusing sort of way.

By the way, does anyone else actually remember Nahlakh?

